{
  "dom_id": "step-q115460",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{7x+2}\\le 2x\\cdot {3}^{7x+3}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{7x+2} & \\le 2x\\cdot {3}^{7x+3} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{7x+2}-2x\\cdot {3}^{7x+3} & \\le 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x\\cdot 3)\\cdot {3}^{7x+2} & \\le 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)\\cdot {3}^{7x+2} & \\le 0 \\\\ x(x-6)\\cdot {3}^{7x+2} & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${3}^{7x+2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx(x-6)\\le 0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=0$ and $x=6.$ The parabola is below the $x$-axis or touch this axis when $0\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013930/assets/d75b387ac3e54f253eab62d9492b39f2744298e776f663d542b26e3c47f7d6a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $0\\le x\\le 6.$",
